  "id": "chapter2_activecode-exercises",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "chapter2_activecode-exercises.html",
  "type": "Exercises",
  "number": "2.14",
  "title": "Activecode Exercises",
  "body": " Activecode Exercises  Answer the following Activecode questions to assess what you have learned in this chapter.    Fix the code below so that it runs without errors. Hint: you might need to change the names of some variables.   #include <iostream> int main() { char true = 'T'; char false = 'F'; std::cout << true << \" is short for true. \"; std::cout << false << \" is short for false.\" << std::endl; }    Below is one way to fix the program. true and false are keywords, so they cannot be used as variable names.  #include <iostream> int main() { char t = 'T'; char f = 'F'; std::cout << t << \" is short for true. \"; std::cout << f << \" is short for false.\" << std::endl; std::cout << f << \" is short for false.\" << std::endl; }       Finish the code below so that it prints I drive a 2014 Buick Regal   #include <iostream> int main() { std::string make; make = \"Buick\" \/\/ Finish the rest of the assignment statements by assigning \/\/ 2014 and Regal to their respective variables. \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"I drive a \" << year << \" \" << make << \" \"; std::cout << model << std::endl; }    Finish the code below so that it prints I drive a 2014 Buick Regal . Use the lines to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the exercise.    Finish the code below so that it prints I drive a 2014 Buick Regal . Use the lines to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the exercise.     Open main()    Initialize variable make as string    Assign Buick to make;     Initialize year as int;    Initialize year as double;     Assign a number to year;     Initialize model as string;    Initialize model as char;     Assign model name to model;    Output year and make;    Output model name;    Close main()         Fix the code below so that it prints Cady scored 90% on the exam.   #include <iostream> int main() { \/\/ Modify the next line so that Cady = 0.9. int Cady = 3 * 5 * (6 \/ 100); \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"Cady scored \" << Cady * 100 << \"% on the exam.\"; }    Below is one way to fix the program. We want to use doubles so that our result isn't rounded down to 0 through integer division.  #include <iostream> int main() { double Cady = 3 * 5 * (6 \/ 100.0); std::cout << \"Cady scored \" << Cady * 100 << \"% on the exam.\"; }      Finish the code below so that it returns the correct volume of a sphere. Hint: think about what happens when you use integer division. The volume of a sphere is given by:   #include <iostream> int main() { int radius = 5; double pi = 3.14; \/\/ Use these variables and the formula for volume to complete the next line. volume = \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"Your solution had volume = \" << volume << std::endl; std::cout << \"The correct solution has volume = 104.667\"; }    Use the lines on to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the exercise.     Open main()    Initialize and assign radius;    Initialize and assign pi;     Initialize volume as double;    Initialize volume as int;     Calculate volume with spherical volume formula;    Output calculated volume;    Close main()         Fix the code below so that assigns a its correct value of 'a' . Hint: use character operations!   #include <iostream> int main() { char a = 's'; \/\/ Fix the line below. Do NOT change the numbers! Instead, \/\/ change the location of the parentheses. a = a - 3 * 4 + (1 + 3); \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << a; }   Below is one way to complete the program. There are many creative ways that you could use the order of operations to come up with a complex expression that will bring you to 'a', here is one way.  #include <iostream> int main() { char a = 's'; a = a - (3 * (4 + 1) + 3); std::cout << a; }       Write code that assigns apples to the variable oranges, and oranges to the variable apples, then swaps their values. Be sure to include any necessary headers. YOU MAY NOT HARDCODE YOUR SOLUTION.   int main() { \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"Your solution had apples = \" << apples; std::cout << \"and oranges = \" << oranges << \".\" << std::endl ; std::cout << \"The correct solution has apples = apples, and oranges = oranges.\"; }    Use the lines to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the exercise.     Open main()    Initialize oranges as string;    Assign \"apples\" to oranges;    Initialize apples as string;    Assign \"oranges\" to apples;    Assign apples to a temporary variable;    Assign oranges to apples;    Assign the temporary variable to oranges;    Output apples and oranges;    Close main()         Write code that prints Eat , More , and Chicken on 3 consecutive lines. Be sure to inclue any necessary headers.   int main() { }   Below is one way to implement the solution.  #include <iostream> int main() { std::cout << \"Eat\" << std::endl; std::cout << \"More\" << std::endl; std::cout << \"Chicken\" << std::endl; }      Write code that calculates how much you you will spend after tipping 20% on your $36.25 dinner. Save the result of this calculation in plus_tip . Be sure to include any necessary headers.   int main() { \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"Your solution had plus_tip = \" << plus_tip << std::endl ; std::cout << \"The correct solution has plus_tip = 43.5\"; }    Use the lines to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the exercise.     Open main()    Initialize and assign $36.25 to price;     Initialize and assign 1.20 to tip;    Initialize and assign .20 to tip;     Calculate plus_tip using tip and price;    Output plus_tip;    Close main()         You have about three hours and fifteen minutes of homework to do today. Rather than starting it right away, you choose to procrastinate by calculating how many seconds you'll be spending on your work. Convert the time to seconds and store the result in seconds . Be sure to inclue any necessary headers.   int main() { \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << \"Your solution had seconds = \" << seconds << std::endl ; std::cout << \"The correct solution has seconds = 11700\"; }   Below is one way to implement the solution.  #include <iostream> int main() { int hours = 3; int minutes = 15; int total_minutes = minutes + 60 * hours; int seconds = total_minutes * 60; }      Write code that calculates and prints the average of a and b if a = 3.14, and b = 1.59. You may only use one line of code. Be sure to inclue any necessary headers.   int main() { \/\/ DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING BELOW THIS LINE. std::cout << std::endl ; std::cout << \"Your program should have printed 2.365\"; }    Write code that calculates and prints the average of a and b if a = 3.14, and b = 1.59. You may only use one line of code. Use the lines on the to construct the pseudocode, then go back to complete the Activecode.     Open main()    Output the expression for average of 2 numbers;    Close main()       "
