  "number": "5.2.7",
  "title": "Looking Ahead: Behavior That Belongs to an Object",
  "body": " Looking Ahead: Behavior That Belongs to an Object  So far, functions that operate on structures have been separate from the structure definitions themselves.  For example:  double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle); void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle);  These are called free functions . The structure object is supplied as an argument.  But both operations are closely related to a Rectangle . C++ also allows functions to become part of the structure itself. Such functions are called member functions .  In the next section, we will see how:  calculate_area(room);  can become:  room.calculate_area();  and how:  double_size(room);  can become:  room.double_size();  "
