  "body": " Check Your Understanding    Which declaration correctly declares a constructor for a class named Book ?     void Book();   A constructor has no return type, not even void .     Book();   A constructor has the same name as the class and no return type.     book();   Constructor names must match the class name exactly.     Book Book();   A constructor does not specify a return type.        Given these constructor declarations, which constructor is called by Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0}; ?  Rectangle(); Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);     Rectangle()   The no-argument constructor does not match the two arguments supplied when room is created.      Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)    The two supplied double values match the two-parameter constructor.     Both constructors   One matching constructor is selected for the construction.        What is the purpose of the initializer list in this constructor?  Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year) : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) { }      It initializes the data members from the constructor arguments.    The initializer list initializes title and year as the object is constructed.      It declares two new local variables named title and year .     title and year are data members of the object.      It returns the completed Book object.    Constructors do not return objects with a return statement.        Arrange the blocks to define the two-parameter constructor for Book outside the class using a member initializer list.  class Book { public: Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year); private: std::string title; int year; };     Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year)    : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) {    }    void Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year)    return Book;       Complete the two constructor definitions for Book . The default constructor should create an untitled book from year 0. The second constructor should initialize the object from its arguments.   #include <iostream> #include <string> class Book { public: Book(); Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year); void report() const; private: std::string title; int year; }; int main() { Book unknown; Book novel{\"The Left Hand of Darkness\", 1969}; unknown.report(); novel.report(); return 0; } Book::Book() \/\/ Complete the initializer list. { } Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year) \/\/ Complete the initializer list. { } void Book::report() const { std::cout << title << \" (\" << year << \")\\n\"; }     Initialize title and year after a colon and before the constructor body.    Book::Book() : title(\"Untitled\"), year(0) { } Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year) : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) { }    "
